        "source":  "MIT Technology Review",
        "url":  "https://www.technologyreview.com/2026/10/02/1145639/dont-be-fooled-llms-dont-reason/",
        "date":  "2026-10-02",
        "theme":  "Philosophy",
        "summary":  "A former member of the AlphaGo team argues that current large language models perform sophisticated pattern completion rather than genuine reasoning, using AlphaGo\u0027s move 37 as a case study in true machine deliberation. The author contends that AlphaGo\u0027s success came from combining fast intuition networks with slow deliberative search — a dual-system architecture that today\u0027s LLMs lack. The piece calls for AI systems that maintain explicit epistemic states and separable knowledge representations to achieve genuine reasoning.",
        "execSummary":  "This opinion piece delivers a technically grounded challenge to the enterprise assumption that current LLMs reason through problems — they pattern-match at scale, which produces reasoning-like outputs but fails in novel or high-stakes scenarios requiring genuine deliberation. For enterprise leaders deploying AI agents in complex decision workflows, LLM-based agents are reliable for well-patterned tasks but remain unreliable for genuinely novel situations. The architectural argument — that future AI needs separable intuition and deliberation systems — suggests the next meaningful capability leap for enterprise AI will come from hybrid architectures, not just larger models. Organizations should factor this reliability ceiling into risk assessments for autonomous decision-making use cases.",
        "vendorLean":  "Neutral",
